Absolutely. In programming, data types determine what kind of value a variable can store. For example, a variable can store text, numbers, Boolean values, collections, dates, objects, and many other types of data.

There is no single universal list of all data types because different programming languages define their own types. However, below is a comprehensive classification of the major data types used across programming languages.

### 1\. Primitive data types

These are the basic data types found in many programming languages.

| Data type        | What it stores                 | Example         |
| ---------------- | ------------------------------ | --------------- |
| Integer (int)    | Whole numbers                  | 25              |
| Float (float)    | Decimal numbers                | 12.5            |
| Double (double)  | High-precision decimal numbers | 3.14159265      |
| Boolean (bool)   | True or false                  | true            |
| Character (char) | A single character             | 'A'             |
| String (str)     | Text                           | "Hello"         |
| Byte (byte)      | Small integer values           | 255             |
| Short (short)    | Small whole numbers            | 120             |
| Long (long)      | Large whole numbers            | 9876543210      |
| Void (void)      | No return value                | void function() |

